EXT. CATTLE PEN AT DUSK - SUNDAY

Dust. Lowing steers. A split-rail fence, a bucket, a lantern on a post.

HANK YAZZIE, forties, leans on the fence. MILLIE, thirties, a cook in a flour-dusted apron, ladles beans from a pot. LAZARUS, sixty, a one-eyed drover, sits on an upturned bucket whittling. PETE, nineteen, new on the crew, brushes a horse.

MILLIE
(to everyone)
Supper's up.

HANK
Smells good.

LAZARUS
Smells like boots.

MILLIE
Then don't eat it, Laz.

LAZARUS
I'll eat it.

PETE
Mrs. Millie, can I have seconds?

MILLIE
You haven't had firsts.

PETE
I'm planning ahead.

HANK
Pete, how's the bay mare?

PETE
Limping, Mr. Yazzie.

HANK
Which leg?

PETE
Left front.

LAZARUS
It's the right.

PETE
It's the left.

LAZARUS
(muttering)
Boy can't tell his left from his own elbow.

MILLIE
Lazarus.

LAZARUS
I'm just saying.

HANK
Look at the hoof, Pete. Look, not guess.

PETE
Yes, sir.

He crouches. Lifts the mare's foot. Squints.

PETE (CONT'D)
Stone.

HANK
Pick it out.

LAZARUS
Right front. Told you.

PETE
Left.

LAZARUS
Right!

HANK
Everyone eat.

They quiet. Millie passes bowls. The steers settle. Lazarus slowly resumes whittling.
